> Yup. ;-} It'll be a few years before 64-bit addressing becomes relevant on
> the desktop.

"The desktop," of course, is not a monolithic market, and it's hard to
know what drives it.  Nearly twenty years ago, when the state of the
art in the desktop market was a 16-bit PC-AT running at a few
megahertz with 640 K of memory, a programmer asked me rhetorically why
anyone would need more processor power in a desktop.  My answer was
that, if nothing else, a desire for better and faster graphics would
drive the need for PC bandwidth for a long time.  It was easy for me
to see that need: I had watched a Cray-I huff and puff to fill very
expensive frame buffers to make one of the earliest hollywood movies
to use 3-D computer generated animation.

My prediction was, if I may say so, pretty accurate, but something
interesting has happened along the way: graphics cards have become at
least as powerful and sophisticated as the CPU.  Graphics engines have
leap-frogged the CPU and process data 128 bits at a time and often
have faster memory with more sophisticated bus architecture than the
main board.

At the same time, though, "desktops" have become so powerful that they
have crept into the workstation and server market, where 4 gigabytes
of memory is already starting to feel a bit cramped.  And it is easy
to imagine that, with sufficiently inexpensive memory and appropriate
software, managers in even modest-sized enterprises with a need to get
their arms around mountains of data would find a flat 64-bit address
space pretty attractive (even if they had no idea why).

Once the commodity processor makers have figured out how to make
64-bit processors available to the market at commodity processor
prices, there will be no shortage of applications.


