"Robert Myers" <rmyers1400@attbi.com> wrote in message
news:461b05a6.0203202052.217b4a8@posting.google.com...

...

> Once the commodity processor makers have figured out how to make
> 64-bit processors available to the market at commodity processor
> prices, there will be no shortage of applications.

For workstations (as you mentioned), but still not for 'commodity
desktops' - for a long time yet.  And therein lies the problem:  the only
way to make 64-bit processors available at commodity processor prices is to
build them in similar quantities, but without any comparable demand...

AMD may have solved the problem by moving to a 64-bit architecture that will
be their 32-bit product as well.  That will allow 64-bit applications to
'ooze' into the market when and where needed.  Itanium, by contrast, hasn't
a prayer of doing the same thing, at least until such time as its support
for 32-bit applications is completely revamped.

- bill





