"Nathan J. Williams" wrote:
> * I have always been annoyed at documents that use a local hierarchy
>   of section and page numbers instead of a global, linear page
>   order. The AARM, with four levels of hierarchy, is one of the worst
>   offenders I've encountered.

While I never liked the Alpha ARM organization, the hierarchy had a 
reason:  Pages get added.  Lots of them in the early days.  Enough
so that those of use with internal copies had the ARM in a loose-leaf
notebook.  The hierarchy meant that often the index was no more broken
after an addition than it was before.  

Alas, there are many other reasons to dislike the organization of that
manual -- For me it was annoying that the instructions were arranged
by theme rather than alphabetically.  The theme organization made it
an easier bed-time read as you went through it the first few times, but
as a reference, the document organization left something to be desired.

Finally, the three-platform strategy (there's a first -- alpha and 
strategy in the same posting that isn't tounge-in-cheek) pretty much
said
that many aspects of the machine that are important to processor 
designers were tri-modal (or at least aware of the three modes).  
Fortunately, there weren't gross differences between the three at the
hardware level (other than the address translation considerations and
a few control register gnits).  

						matt



