Kriston <Kriston@newsguy.com> writes:

> How can I remove an advfs set?  I have gotten details on the file
> domain and all that jazz, but is there an acceptable way to just
> move all the files onto one partition, break the set, and then use
> ufs instead?  Using Solaris I'd just remove the mount point and
> restart using direct ufs mounts of the mirrored volumes.  How about
> DEC AdvFS?

See man advfs and man rmfset

As a fileset is one of probably more sets on a domain consisting
of one ore ore volumes you'll have to read

man rmfdmn or man rmvol as well.

If there is only one filset in the filedomain and this domain
is on one volume you'll probably ok with

df -k /path                     # find domain and filset_to_remove
ls -lR /etc/fdmns/domain        # list the device
umount /path
rmfset domain filset_to_remove
rmvol domain 

Then you're able to use newfs on the device - but you maybe 
have to edit athe disklabel as it could be marked in use by advfs..

But why?

