Cthulhu wrote:
> 
> Jeff Campbell <jcampbell@ins-msi.com> wrote:
> 
> > DIMMS. The machine has a limit of 2 sets of 64 MB pairs. Each memory
> > socket pair is mapped on modulo 512 MB centers.
> 
> Ah, now I think I understand... so, up to 512MB, I can not use more than
> the first DIMM slot on each memory card, right?

No, each of the 8 memory socket pairs can contain DIMMs sized upto 512
MB,
64, 128, 256, or 512. The machine supports 4 GB of physical memory (8
times 512).

> 
> > The sparse mapping array
> > is larger the 4GB when the third xxx MB DIMM pair is installed.
> 
> What is the "sparse mapping array", and why it should address more that
> 4GB if that is the max. memory that machine can support? :?

The memory mapping scheme used on the 1200 allows holes in the physical
addressing of memory. As an example, your current configuration has 2
sets
of DIMMs. The 1st pair has 64 MB but the address range for that socket
pair is 0-512 MB. The 2nd pair of 64 MB in the 2nd socket pair likewise
only fills 1/8th of the second address range - 512-1024 MB.

The sparse memory map maps out the missing physical memory. An entry is
needed in this table for every missing longword (quadword?). It's this
table size that is larger than 4 GB if more than 2 pairs of 32 MB DIMMs
are used.

