Nick Deinhardt wrote:
> 
> Can anybody tell me where I can find information on replacing the hard
> disks on a Alphastation 255. They are very noisy and the loudness is
> increasing. They might die soon. Also does anybody know how I can
> easily transfer the current redhat linux installation on to the new
> disks (I would love to just copy the disks). It would be great if I
> could save from re-installing everything.
> 
> Thanks nick

If you do not plan on running OpenVMS or DEC/Tru64 UNIX, just about any
50-pin narrow SCSI drive should work... Assuming you're using the
onboard SCSI controller. A Seagate ST3xx7xN would do nicely. Preferably
Cheetah or Barracuda. Medalist line would be ok, but tend to be a little
slow on multiple accesses. And the 10K RPM drives tend to be hot and
noisy little buggers (that and it's a waste with the onboard
controller). I myself have been eyeing ST34371N drives that show up on
E-bay every so often.

As for transferring an install image, there's several ways. If you end
up purchasing the exact same drives as you have now, you could simply dd
them across. Otherwise, I like to tar up the original drive, install a
minimal instance of the OS on the new drive to get the boot block and
partitions right, then untar the old data on top of the new drive. 

Then again, perhaps it's a good opportunity to upgrade the OS to the
latest and greatest... Just pull across the user-specific data after
doing a full install.

-- 
