Uh, your last sentence is what makes no sense, to me at least.  Ultrix
wasn't, originally, "System V-based"; it was 32V-based.  The exact
problem is that it later integrated something or other that came from
a later AT&T Unix that's not covered by the current free license.

Ultrix-32 was, more or less, rebadged 4.2BSD.  However, at least by
version 3, purchasing a full source distribution required proving to
DEC that one had an AT&T *System V* source license.  A 32V license
was not sufficient.  I went around and around on this and I'm quite
certain that it's true.

Perhaps the only System V code was in the "System V" versions of certain
programs such as the shell which were provided for compatibility reasons.
Certainly the bits of the kernel I've looked at show no obvious System V
influence.  However, DEC didn't sell just kernel source -- it was the
whole source distribution or nothing at all.

