>it never happened but continue to perform installation.  I narrowed down 
>to program called 'finder'.  I had selected 3 to enter system management
>to get the root prompt.  Then I executed 'finder -r' but it crashed with
>the same message (mscp_bbr_lock).  It happened at my slower system (450 
>Mhz P3, RH 7.1) at my work.  It never happened on my faster system
>(1.9 GHz P4, RH 7.2).  I suspect compiler bug or so.  It worked fine
>on SIMH emulator.  I am looking for Ultrix 4.x sources to find a bug
>during mscp_bbr_lock routine.  Does anyone have Ultrix sources?

BBR is Bad Block Replacement.  A routine, mscp_bbr_lock(), is called
by a number of routines in the MSCP support.  The commentary is a
bit sparse, but I'd guess that it ensures BBR can only be occuring
once per device.  Early in the routine, gets the address of a "BBRB"
(which you can probably find in the MSCP related include files).  If
this address is NULL, there is no BBR work area allocated to the
parent data structure and the system panics.  Obviously, ULTRIX
believe that such a data structure must be allocated before BBR
can proceed.

A curious question would be why your simulation is triggering BBR
at all.

The ULTRIX sources are covered by a license, since they include
consider code derived from the original AT&T work.

