In article <77b348b9.0203011921.66d6dff9@posting.google.com>,
TiHKAL <msgtihkal@excite.com> wrote:
> Is it possible to install both Tru64unix and Linux on 2 different
> partitions on the same scsi hard drive and be able to choose either os
> at boot? If so, would SRM be the boot loader or milo? I seem to
> remember seeing somewhere that SRM only supported one os per hard
> disk, but I'm not sure if this is true.

To do this you need to have a partition scheme that both operating systems
wil use. It appears Linux supports the BSD partitions that SRM uses, and
SRM does support specifying a boot partition.

There's a couple of ways to approach this problem, then:

	1. Boot from a linux kernel in the Tru64 "a" partition that has
	   been configured to mount a later partition from "d" onwards
	   ("a" is the BSD root, "b" is the BSD swap, and "c" is the whole
	   disk) as its root.

	2. Install the Linux root in a high partition ("d" etc) and get SRM
	   to boot that.

In either case you should be able to use the same swap partition for both
operating systems. So here's a possible layout:

	part	linux	tru64	

	a	hda0	rz0a	BSD (Tru64) root partition
	b	hda1	rz0b	Swap partition
	c	hda2	rz0c	"Whole Disk" partition, do not touch.	
	d	hda3	rz0d	Linux root partition
	e..h	hda4...	rz0e...	other Tru64 and Linux partitions.

You'll have to do the linux-fu yourself, but it'll end up
something like this:

>>>boot dka0 -fi vmlinuz -fl "root=/dev/hda4 bootdevice=hda4"

Or:

>>>boot dka0 -fi 4/vmlinuz -fl "root=/dev/hda4 bootdevice=hda4"

to boot Linux, and:

>>>boot dka0 -fi vmunix

to boot Tru64

Good luck!
