Not sure about the specific model of AlphaServer 1200, but at one time I had
some 168-pin DIMMs for Alphas in my possession.  They were ECC, and were rated
at the somewhat odd speed of 83MHz, to match the Alpha equivalent of the Wintel
front side bus.  They worked splendidly in Wintel computers designed for PC66
memory, because they were certified to run at a faster clocking.

Carrying this reasoning further, if the DIMMs in your system are also 168-pin,
you would probably succeed with commodity PC100 ECC registered DIMMs.  Once
again, PC100 would be rated faster than 83MHz.  PC100 ECC registered DIMMs are
more expensive than the common PC100 non-ECC DIMMs, but they would also be MUCH
less expensive than branded DEC Alpha DIMMs... Ben Myers

